findings.body:
Authorization allows the request if the user is the organizer OR an attendee:

```ts
const isAttendee = !!booking.attendees.find((attendee) => attendee.email === user.email);
if (!isTeamAdminOrOwner && !isOrganizer && !isAttendee) { ... }
```

Combined with the dialog's unrestricted email entry, any original invitee can inject any third-party email into the booking, getting them full calendar details, organizer info, other attendees, and a meeting invitation without the organizer's consent. The organizer should normally be the only one (plus team admins/owners) permitted to add guests. This is a serious privacy/access-control issue.

The dedupe/blacklist filter compares emails with strict equality:

```ts
const uniqueGuests = guests.filter(
    (guest) =>
      !booking.attendees.some((attendee) => guest === attendee.email) &&
      !blacklistedGuestEmails.includes(guest)
);
```

Email addresses are case-insensitive per RFC 5321. Submitting `Test@Example.com` while an existing attendee is `test@example.com` will not be filtered out, so a duplicate attendee row is created (or causes a unique-constraint failure, surfaced to the user). Both the dedupe check and the blacklist comparison must lowercase values first; same applies to `newGuests.includes(attendee.email)` in `sendAddGuestsEmails` which controls whether new guests get the original 'scheduled' email vs the 'guest added' email.

The handler instantiates the EventManager with the calling session user and their credentials:

```ts
const credentials = await getUsersCredentials(ctx.user);
const eventManager = new EventManager({ ...user, credentials: [...credentials] });
await eventManager.updateCalendarAttendees(evt, booking);
```

When a team admin or attendee (or anyone authorized by the buggy permission check) adds guests, `user` is the requester, not `booking.userId` (the organizer). `updateCalendarAttendees` will then use the requester's calendar integrations to push the updated invite, which can fail, target the wrong calendar, or succeed only partially. Credentials must come from `booking.userId` (or whichever organizer owns the destination calendar).

```ts
try {
  await sendAddGuestsEmails(evt, guests);
} catch (err) {
  console.log("Error sending AddGuestsEmails");
}
```

The exception is dropped (no `logger.error`, no context, no rethrow). The handler still returns `{ message: "Guests added" }`, so the client toasts success and the user assumes guests were notified. At minimum, use `logger.error` with the error and rethrow or return a structured failure so the UI can surface it.

```ts
...calendarEvent.attendees.map((attendee) => {
  if (newGuests.includes(attendee.email)) {
    return sendEmail(() => new AttendeeScheduledEmail(calendarEvent, attendee));
  } else {
    return sendEmail(() => new AttendeeAddGuestsEmail(calendarEvent, attendee));
  }
})
```

`AttendeeScheduledEmail` is the first-touch booking confirmation containing reschedule/cancel links and language framing as if the recipient originally booked it. Newly added guests did not book anything; sending them this template is misleading and may confuse them about whether they own the booking. They should receive `AttendeeAddGuestsEmail` (or a dedicated 'you have been added' template). Additionally, every existing attendee also receives a duplicate `AttendeeAddGuestsEmail` with `icalEvent` `method: REQUEST`, which will trigger another 'invitation' notification in their calendar.
